    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my thesis chapter regarding the Maillard reaction and I'm stuck on the kinetic details. Can you explain the specific role that amino acids and reducing sugars play in the initial stages of the reaction, and how fluctuations in pH levels specifically affect the rate of non-enzymatic browning in protein-rich substrates?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the Maillard reaction, amino acids and reducing sugars form complex intermediates that facilitate browning. Amino acids, specifically lysine, histidine, and arginine, react to form heterocyclic compounds, contributing to the initial stages of browning. Reducing sugars, such as glucose and fructose, react with amino acids to form compounds that can deaminate and rehydrate, further driving the Maillard reaction. Fluctuations in pH levels can impact the rate of non-enzymatic browning by influencing the availability of functional groups and enzymatic activity, with a decrease in pH typically favoring the Maillard reaction.",
      "source": "target",
